Date: Tue, 28 Dec 93 00:36:54 EST
From: trader@cellar.org
Subject: Thinking about thinking

I note that one of the "goals" of the group is the exploration of the
"technological extension of human intelligence".  While this is a topic
that greatly interests me, my casual perusal of the lists postings has
lead me to the conclusion that "technological extension" doesn't seem
to go very far beyond the discussion of nootropics or uploading.
 
Again, while these are topics that interest me, I don't see much
usefulness in increasing one's "intelligence" through smart drugs or
mainlining on-line databases without a fundamental understanding of
thinking (information processing, analysis, hypothesis formulation, etc).
 
In the off-line (hardcopy) world, the only resources I've encountered and
found to be of any value are certain titles by Edward deBono ( _I am
Right, You are Wrong_, _deBono's Thinking Course_, and _Practical
Thinking_ are three that I've found particularly useful).
 
In the on-line world I've found nothing ((ab)Usenet primarily, though
I've found little to nothing utilizing other Internet services).
rec.org.mensa was useless, and sci.cognitive seemed oriented more
toward the theoretical than the practical.
 
So is there anyone else on this list exploring- or learned in- the
understanding/analysis of thinking processes that can point me toward
some other resources?
 
It seems that Geoff Dale (and others) may have touched on this previously
(in a discussion regarding Sufism) but- while I would not discount the book
outright- I tend to shy away from anything even remotely spiritualistic
or that deals with motivation as the primary source (or obstacle) of
learning.  The motivation's there- that's why I'm reading and looking
for more.  I'm looking almost exclusively for the practical and systemic.
 
 
btw, if anyone should feel the need to converse with me in e-mail, this
is my first post to the list and the list is gatewayed on my system.
(Don't worry, access is restricted to those who have subscribed to the
list.)  Thus, I don't know what the "From" line is going to look like and
one should exercise reasonable caution.
 
thanks


