Date: Tue, 28 Dec 93 00:26:39 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Atomic Genes

(Why does this thread name sound like the title of a 1950s pulp SF thriller?)
I sense some confusion, in part about nomenclature and in part about genetics;
perhaps I can clarify a little, though my expertise is limited to a recent
upper-division undergraduate genetics class.  I'll use some computer
analogies, and will talk about human beings, though -- with modest numbers
of caveats -- most of what I will say goes for most other plants and animals.
 
Genetics has to do with data stored in the DNA of an organism.  In principle,
that DNA has a memory map, just like any other memory store.  A location
in the memory map is called a gene; the data stored there is called an
allele.  These latter two terms are often confused, and dangerously so.
Thus we might say that location so-and-so in the map is the gene for
eye color (actually, one of several such genes), and that in the case of
such-and-such an individual, the allele stored there codes for "blue".
 
Human DNA is organized into physically distinct chunks called chromosomes.
Most of us have forty-six, comprising two each of twenty-two different kinds,
and two additional "sex chromosomes", which are the same kind for garden-
variety females (both "X") and different for garden-variety males ("X" and
"Y").  Let's forget sex chromosomes for the moment.
 
The fact that there are two each of most of the kinds of chromosomes will
suggest, correctly, that we each contain not one but two almost-complete
DNA memory spaces.  Thus in the example given, we each contain not just
one but two genes coding for eye color, at the same places in each of
the two copies of chromosome whatever.  The alleles located at these two
memory positions; however, need not be the same.
 
Sexual reproduction mixes up alleles pretty well, for at least three
reasons.  First, each new individual gets genes from two other individuals;
one of each kind of chromosome from mom and one from dad.  (Canonical
oddity -- those sex chromosomes again.)  Second, the choice of which
chromosome of each pair, gets passed on to an offspring, is random
for each pair of chromosomes; that is, each garden-variety human being
is capable in principle of producing 2**23 genetically distinct sperm
cells (if male) or eggs (if female).  Third, there's a mechanism for
swapping data between corresponding positions in the two memory spaces
present in each individual.
 
What alleles do is code for the production of proteins, and what proteins
do is subject to all the wiles and weirdnesses of biochemistry.  What the
term "atomic genes" suggests to me is simple Mendelian inheritance, which
occurs in nature often enough to be useful to study.  What happens here is
that some observable trait in a critter is determined by exactly one gene,
and there are only two possible alleles known to reside at that location,
and if both should be present in a single individual, one will "win"
completely.
 
For example, take flower color in certain plants.  Suppose a plant has
genes that code for a biochemical factory to produce red pigment --
potentially this would take lots of genes -- but suppose that one of
the alleles in question -- call it "R" (for red) also occurs in nature
in a "broken" form (call it lower-case "r"), which produces a protein
that does not work in the factory.  Remember that the plant has two
copies of its memory map, so there are three combinations of alleles it
can have at the place where "R" and "r" go; namely "RR", "Rr" (which
is equivalent to "rR", and "rr".  As long as the plant has at least one
"R" allele, its factory will work, and it will be red, but the "rr" plant
will not produce red pigment and will have unpigmented flowers (which are
in essence white).  Thus "RR" and "Rr" yield indistinguishable plants.
However, the offspring of those plants will be statistically distinguishable,
for the "RR" will necessarily pass an "R" on to each offspring, whereas
the "Rr" can pass on either an "R" or an "r".

That's the kind of genetics that tends to show up in high school biology
courses.  There's more, however.  There are genes that are additive, there
are alleles that don't work at all unless some other alleles are present,
there are physical traits that are produced in a complicated way by many
interacting genes, and so on.  There are even genes that kill you --
one doesn't see many "dominant lethals" -- that is, alleles for which even
one copy is fatal -- but we were told that on the average, each human being
carried something like six to ten "recessive lethals" -- alleles which
would kill if you had two of them, bbut you don't.

Genetics was a fun course, but I shouldn't blather on about it any more.
Hope this exposition helps.

                                       -- Jay Freeman

