From: "Phil G. Fraering" <pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu>
Subject: POLI: Governments as fat, juicy targets

Amara writes:

   This reminds me of my favorite argument for anarchy and why you don't 
   have to worry much about a large defense force.  Imagine the U.S.
   not as one country but as dozens and dozens of little enclaves trading
   with each other.  If a country wanted to "invade" the US, what would
   they invade?  There wouldn't be a Washington D.C. fat, juicy target or
   smaller state government centers. The invaders would be up against 
   mostly individuals, who had probably given some thought to self and 
   local defense.  It would be too expensive (manpower and money-wise) to
   overtake the (enclaves in the) US in such a situation.


I agree with you about the part well-armed individuals would play,
but that only works if you're dealing with a rational opponent or
one that isn't planning to just wipe out the native population. I
think if the insurance companies don't band together and build something
like strategic defense, the anarchy won't last long.

(Of course, the government, for whatever reason, is unwilling to put
a serious effort into such defenses. So I can't use this as a reason
why government would be better than anarchy.)


