From: cards@top.cis.syr.edu
Subject: Thinking about thinking

I would strongly suggest one of the works on the Extropians
reading list: _The New Inquisition: Irrational Rationalism and
the Citadel of Science_, by Robert Anton Wilson.  Another
must-read would be any freshman text on logic (not Boolean
digital logic, but Aristotelian classical logic, modern
class (set-based) logic, etc.).  Unfortunately most schools
don't make a course in logic a requirement anymore, and
even those of us who have taken such a course probably
did so in our pre-rational days.

I also experiment with positive thinking, psychocybernetics,
self-hypnosis, meditation, yoga, t'ai-chi-chuan, psi exercises
and magic.  I plan to check out Crowley's Magick, Madame
Blavatsky's Theosophy, Gurdjieff's writings and Sufism
(I have already read _The Sufis_ by Idries Shah and found
it fascinating).  Do many of these sound irrelevant to
your question? Maybe so, mebbe not: although I value
rational thought highly, I do not believe it to be the
ONLY useful mode.  Oh yeah, I used to do Zen, but after
achieving what I believed (in the absence of a roshi)
to be kensho (minor satori, or enlightment), I decided
that it was a dead end which led to inaction.

I have a neat book (got it as a premium for subscribing
to Scientific American, several years ago) called
_aha! Gotcha: Paradoxes to puzzle and delight_ by
Martin Gardner.  There are lots of such books around.
I'm not sure they explicate thinking, but they provide
lots of little creative or analytic or synergetic
exercises.

Finally, a technique I use: start with a statement or
question and try to trace back (ALL the way to their
ULTIMATE antecedents) the premises.  The only way I
can keep track of this is with a graph.  It starts
out as a tree, but then some cycles appear after a
while.

Was this along the lines intended?
